WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
How do you test one protein inside a crowded cell?

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.700
Copy its gene and put it into a foreign cell that normally lacks it.

3
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.500
Let the host build it, then watch what response appears.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.060
That method is heterologous expression.

5
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:21.233
Heterologous simply means foreign.

6
00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:26.033
You make a DNA copy of an expressed gene, called cDNA.

7
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:29.413
Or you start directly with its RNA.

8
00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:35.293
Then you deliver those instructions into a host cell lacking that response.

9
00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:41.993
Common hosts are cultured HEK293 cells and frog Xenopus oocytes.

10
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:46.463
The host builds the protein and puts it in its membrane.

11
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:52.903
Now add a stimulus, and compare with an untransfected cell carrying no added gene.

12
00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:57.403
The control trace stays flat, while the test cell responds.

13
00:00:58.863 --> 00:01:04.303
Heterologous expression tests molecular sufficiency: whether one protein, in a

14
00:01:04.303 --> 00:01:06.263
foreign cell, produces a response.

15
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.053
Now test a classic trap.

16
00:01:10.353 --> 00:01:16.013
You inject RNA for an unknown membrane protein into frog oocytes, and chloride

17
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:21.413
current appears. Water-injected oocytes from the same frog stay flat.

18
00:01:22.713 --> 00:01:25.773
Does that prove your protein is itself a chloride channel?

19
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:37.787
Not yet. Oocytes carry chloride channels of their own, and foreign proteins can

20
00:01:37.787 --> 00:01:43.687
switch them on. Good experiments include water-injected or untransfected controls,

21
00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:45.847
and match surface expression.

22
00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:49.567
Otherwise, different amounts alone can mislead.

23
00:01:50.887 --> 00:01:55.987
To show one protein is enough to form the pathway, purify it into a lipid bilayer

24
00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:57.267
with no other proteins.

25
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:00.387
Two limits keep results honest.

26
00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:04.867
First, no current above background does not tell you why.

27
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:10.667
A mutant protein may be absent, mislocalized, or unable to conduct.

28
00:02:12.107 --> 00:02:15.527
Second, a response in a foreign cell shows only sufficiency.

29
00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:21.487
Testing whether an intact animal needs the gene requires removing it there.

30
00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.567
Even so, the method unlocked major discoveries.

31
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:33.307
In 1997, Caterina, Julius and colleagues put pools of rodent sensory-neuron genes

32
00:02:33.307 --> 00:02:40.067
into HEK cells. They watched for calcium entering when they added capsaicin, the

33
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:45.687
chilli burn. Splitting a responding pool left one gene: TRPV1.

34
00:02:45.687 --> 00:02:51.967
In those foreign cells, its channel also opened above about 43 °C.

35
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:59.020
In 2006, Suh, Inoue, Meyer and Hille expressed potassium channels in foreign cells.

36
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.260
Depleting one membrane lipid, PIP2, made the current fall, so lipid loss alone was

37
00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:12.520
enough. In 2020, Shui and colleagues paired Xenopus oocytes carrying two worm

38
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.980
junction proteins, called innexins.

39
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.280
Current strongly favoured one direction between the cells.

40
00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:23.843
Moving one gene into a foreign cell strips away the crowd.

41
00:03:25.103 --> 00:03:29.343
With careful controls, it reveals what one protein can do on its own.

42
00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:33.763
Whether an intact animal needs it takes a different test.
